Subject: Korzybski's "E-Prime", also NLP

>>Next step; learn the same suspicion about claims that rely on excluded middle.
> 
> Can you elaborate on that?

Sure.  In the real world, Aristotelian logic is in general false.  Neither the
"Law of the Excluded Middle" (A | ~A), nor noncontradiction ~(A & ~A) holds.

It's become a cliche to trot out QM in support of this, but it's true even
classically (as proponents of fuzzy logic and the related "fudgy logic" in
AI have realized).  To see this for yourself, think carefully about the
predicates "hot" and "cold".

Part of "conscious of abstracting" is the awareness that natural language
tends to impose on our thinking a two-valuedness which is unsane.  GS teaches
this insight not just as an intellectual bagatelle but as an active, constant
corrective towards sanity.
